export default "## Why Cycle Length Changes\n\nA menstrual cycle has two phases. The luteal phase, from ovulation to the next period, is remarkably consistent between cycles for a given person, typically lasting 10 to 16 days. The follicular phase, from the period start to ovulation, varies much more. This is where most cycle length changes originate.\n\nWhen a cycle shortens, it almost always does so because ovulation is happening earlier. The follicular phase has compressed. This can happen for several reasons, some benign and some worth investigating.\n\nThis guide is for educational purposes only. It is not a substitute for medical advice.\n\n## The Most Common Explanations\n\n### Perimenopause\n\nThe most common cause of a consistently shorter cycle in someone over 35 is the beginning of hormonal changes associated with the menopausal transition. As ovarian reserve declines, the pituitary gland sends more FSH to stimulate follicle development. The ovaries respond by maturing follicles faster. The result is earlier ovulation and a shorter overall cycle.\n\nThis process typically starts years before periods become noticeably irregular. A 28-day cycle that quietly shortens to 24 or 25 days over a year or two, with everything else still regular, is often the first perimenopausal signal. Other signs that may accompany it include slightly heavier periods, sleep changes, mood shifts, or the occasional hot flash.\n\n### Thyroid Dysfunction\n\nBoth underactive and overactive thyroid function can alter cycle length. Hypothyroidism more often produces heavier, longer cycles or irregular timing. Hyperthyroidism can shorten cycles or cause them to become lighter and more frequent. If cycle shortening comes with fatigue, weight changes, temperature sensitivity, heart rate changes, or mood effects, thyroid function is worth checking with a TSH blood test.\n\n### Stress and Lifestyle Factors\n\nA demanding period at work, significant emotional stress, or a shift in sleep patterns can alter hypothalamic signaling in ways that change cycle timing. Unlike the more severe stress-induced amenorrhea, which causes missed periods, the effect on cycle length can be subtle. Cycles may arrive a few days earlier than expected across several cycles.\n\nWeight changes, changes in exercise intensity, or starting or stopping hormonal medications can also shift cycle length.\n\n### Natural Variation Within a Person's Baseline\n\nCycle length is not completely fixed across the reproductive lifespan. Many people notice their cycles drift shorter in their 30s compared to their 20s without any identifiable medical cause. A shift of a few days over years, in the absence of other symptoms, may simply be a natural change in your pattern.\n\nThe distinction that matters is a gradual drift over years versus a change that happened over a few months and is continuing to shorten.\n\n## What Shorter Means in Practice\n\nThe clinical threshold for concern is not a specific number but rather your personal change. Someone whose cycles have always been 24 days is not in a different situation than someone with 28-day cycles. Both are normal. But:\n\n- A 28-day cycle that shortens to 22 days in three months is a notable change\n- A 28-day cycle that shortens to 25 days over two years may be age-related drift\n- A cycle that is getting shorter every month with no floor in sight warrants evaluation\n\nTrack cycle length from period start to period start, every cycle. Look at your pattern over six to twelve months before concluding anything about the trend.\n\n## What to Log\n\nFor cycle length changes, your log should capture:\n\n- **Cycle day 1** for every period, which is the first day of full flow\n- **Cycle length** calculated for each completed cycle\n- **Ovulation signs** if you observe them, such as cervical mucus changes, basal body temperature shifts, or ovulation test results, to identify whether earlier ovulation is behind the shorter cycle\n- **Flow changes**, is the shorter cycle also lighter or heavier?\n- **Other symptoms**, fatigue, hot flashes, mood changes, weight shifts, or anything that might point to thyroid or hormonal causes\n\nSix months of detailed logs gives a healthcare provider meaningful data rather than a general complaint of shorter periods.\n\n## Privacy and Cycle Data\n\nCycle length patterns, changes over time, and symptoms like hot flashes or mood shifts are the kind of data that reveal a detailed picture of your reproductive and hormonal health. Cloud-based period trackers store this data on company servers. Flo Health settled an FTC enforcement action in 2021 for sharing period data with Facebook and Google, and a $59.5 million class action settlement followed in 2025.\n\nPeriod tracking data is not protected by HIPAA. In states where reproductive health decisions are criminalized, such data could be accessed by law enforcement through subpoena. An on-device tracker stores cycle data locally. The company cannot access it, and it cannot be obtained remotely.\n\n## When to See a Healthcare Provider\n\n- Cycle length has shortened by five or more days from your established baseline, consistently over three or more cycles\n- You are in your 30s or 40s and the shortening is accompanied by other symptoms such as sleep changes, mood shifts, heavier flow, or anything suggesting perimenopause\n- Cycle length is continuing to shorten with no stabilization\n- You are trying to conceive and a shorter cycle has affected your ovulation timing or luteal phase length\n- The shortening is accompanied by other new symptoms that suggest a systemic cause";
